I'm trying to track down the cause(s) of some SCSI errors that I'm
getting. Almost always the errors occur at night and either show up as
syslog entries or as a completely hung machine (no ping, no login, no
SysRq). I determined that last night, the errors occurred towards the
end of my writing a 1.7GB backup file from root partition (/dev/sda4) to
an IDE partition (/dev/hda1).

So far, I have (just) taken the following steps:

0. Read the "Linux 2.4 SCSI subsystem HOWTO" (dated 2002-04-07)

1. Enabled SCSI logging with

     echo "scsi log timeout 3" > /proc/scsi/scsi

   Are there more useful parameters to use, keeping in mind that my
   /var/log/messages file _is_ on the SCSI disk that's causing problems?

2. Monitoring hourly the number of bad blocks on /dev/sda with

     scu -f /dev/sda show defects

   Currently, the "Defect list length is 5824 bytes, containing 728
   defects" (Is this a lot, little, normal for an 18GB Quantum Atlas 10K
   drive?)

Is there anything else I should or could do to help me track down these
(see below) problems?

Thanks.

